  date        = {2021-02},
  abstract    = {Using smartphone geographical positioning systems (GPS) data
                 for Japan, we show that travel within urban areas frequently
                 occurs along trip chains, involving multiple stops as part of a
                 single journey. Motivated by these empirical findings, we
                 develop a tractable theoretical model of travel itineraries, in
                 which agents choose a set and sequence of locations to visit
                 each day. To overcome the resulting high-dimensionality of the
                 choice set, we develop an approach based on importance
                 sampling. We show that trip chains introduce consumption
                 externalities across locations. We show that these consumption
                 externalities are central to explaining the collapse in foot
