  pages        = {241--263},
  date         = {2000-08},
  abstract     = {Many US cities invest in large public transit projects in
                  order to reduce private vehicle dependence and to reverse the
                  downward trend in public transit use. Using a unique panel
                  data set for five major cities that upgraded their rail
                  transit systems in the 1980s, we estimate new rail transit’s
                  impact on usage and housing values, using distance as a proxy
                  for transit access. New rail transit has a small impact on
                  usage and housing values. This impact is enough to represent
                  tangible benefits of new transit to nearby residents. New
                  transit’s benefits are not uniformly distributed. We document
